Lena — An Emacs major mode for Lean
***********************************

Lena is a set of packages that extend GNU Emacs
(https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/) for the Lean
(https://lean-lang.org) language.  It is a fork of Nael
(https://codeberg.org/mekeor/nael) by Mekeor Melire, which is itself a
radically refactored fork of Lean4-Mode
(https://github.com/leanprover-community/lean4-mode) with focus on
simplicity, modularity and integration into Emacs' core.  Lena adds an
infoview, signs in the margin and more.  See _Lineage and Credits_ for
more information about Lena's lineage.

   The ‘lena’ package defines the major mode.  It also introduces the
‘Lena’ input method
(https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Input-Methods.html)
for typing Unicode characters common in Lean, such as ‘\a’ for ‘α’.
Lena teaches Emacs' built-in LSP
(https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/) client Eglot
(https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/eglot/) about
Lean-specific LSP-extensions
(https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/master/src/Lean/Data/Lsp/Extra.lean),
tells ElDoc
(https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Programming-Language-Doc.html)
to display that information, and shows it in an infoview, like the “info
view
(https://github.com/leanprover/vscode-lean4/blob/master/vscode-lean4/manual/manual.md#infoview)”
in vscode-lean
(https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=leanprover.lean4).
The separate package ‘lena-evil’ binds keys for Evil
(https://github.com/emacs-evil/evil).

   Lena works with Eglot only.  Nael's support for lsp-mode
(https://github.com/emacs-lsp/lsp-mode), its package ‘nael-lsp’, is not
part of Lena.

   *Note*: Much of the code that Lena adds to Nael was written with the
help of AI assistants and reviewed, tested and revised by Lena's
maintainers.

