1.3 What It Adds
================

Compared to Nael 0.8.3:

   • An infoview (‘lena-infoview’, ‘C-c C-i’) that talks to Lean's RPC:
     goals and the expected type, with the changes of the tactic at
     point, folding and filters; subterms described by ElDoc, tooltips
     and the help buffer ‘*Lena Help*’, and followed to their
     definitions; the messages of the line of point, with traces,
     suggestions to apply and links to the explanations of errors.
     Files: ‘lena-rpc.el’, ‘lena-render.el’, ‘lena-infoview.el’,
     ‘lena-infoview-buffer.el’, ‘lena-infoview-hover.el’.
   • Signs in the left margin for diagnostics, complete proofs, unsolved
     goals, uses of ‘sorry’ and lines being processed
     (‘lena-margin-mode’, ‘lena-margin.el’).
   • For Eglot: restarting the current file, which builds its imports
     once (‘lena-eglot-restart-file’, ‘C-c C-r’); not building imports
     when a file is opened (‘lena-eglot-build-dependencies’); Lean's
     silent diagnostics and widgets in messages.
   • Keys for Evil (https://github.com/emacs-evil/evil), in the separate
     package ‘lena-evil’, and a setup for Doom Emacs (see _Installation
     in Doom Emacs_).
   • Smaller changes: a better syntax table, also for ElDoc, and macros
     recognized as such; the first goal is no longer echoed
     (‘lena-eglot-echo-goal’); a shorter ElDoc delay in Lena buffers
     (‘lena-eldoc-idle-delay’).
   • Tests in ‘test/’: ‘emacs -Q --batch -l build.el test’, and
     ‘test-live’ with a Lean installation.
   • Lena needs Emacs 30.1, instead of 29.1.
   • Lena drops Nael's support for lsp-mode (‘nael-lsp’) and uses Eglot
     only.
   • The ‘Lena’ input method replaces Nael's abbreviations (by Alex
     Loitzl, from Mekeor Melire's work on Nael's ‘dev’ branch): ‘\to_1’
     gives ‘→₁’ instead of ‘→_1’, and abbreviations that end in a
     non-word character, such as ‘\d==’, work.


