2 Get Started
*************

Lena is not on Elpa or Melpa yet.  To install Lena, use ‘package-vc’ or
a clone of this repository, see the _Installation_ chapter below.

   Lena, (upstream) Nael and Lean4-Mode each make themselves the major
mode for ‘.lean’ files, so install only one of them.


File: lena.info,  Node: Usage,  Next: Configuration,  Prev: Get Started,  Up: Top

3 Usage
*******

Language Server Protocol (LSP):

   • ‘C-c C-e’ (‘eglot’) starts Eglot, the LSP client.
   • ‘C-c C-r’ (‘lena-eglot-restart-file’) makes the Lean server
     reprocess the current file, building its imports first if they are
     out of date.  Opening a file does not build its imports, unless
     ‘lena-eglot-build-dependencies’ is non-nil.
   • ‘C-c C-i’ (‘lena-infoview’) toggles the infoview, a window that
     shows the goals and the expected type at point and follows point.
     It needs Lean 4.30 or later.
   • ‘C-h .’ (‘eldoc-doc-buffer’) displays the ElDoc documentation
     buffer showing tactic state and expected goal at point, unless the
     infoview shows them.

   Signs in the left margin, while Eglot manages the buffer
(‘lena-margin-mode’):

   • ‘‼’ marks the line of an error, ‘!’ that of a warning or a note.  A
     diagnostic that spans several lines gets a bar over them instead,
     ‘┌’, ‘│’, ‘└’, in its color.  These are the indicators that Flymake
     shows otherwise; Flymake still underlines the diagnostics and shows
     their messages.  The tooltip of a sign shows the message, and
     clicking it lists all diagnostics.
   • ‘✓’ marks a proof that is complete, ‘✗’ the last line of one with
     unsolved goals, where it goes on, and ‘⚠’ a declaration that uses
     ‘sorry’.  Other signs, such as emoji, can be set with
     ‘lena-margin-goals-accomplished’, ‘lena-margin-unsolved-goals’ and
     ‘lena-margin-admitted’; with most fonts, an emoji makes its line
     taller, unless ‘lena-margin-emoji-height’ makes it smaller, such as
     0.7.
   • An orange ‘│’ marks the lines that Lean is still processing.

   Infoview:

   • Inspired by lean.nvim, in a complete proof, a line above the goals
     says "Goals accomplished ✓", and replaces "no goals" when none are
     left.
   • Below the goals and the expected type, the section _Messages_ shows
     Lean's messages on the line of point: errors, warnings, and output
     such as that of ‘#check’.  Messages that start on another line,
     like an error about a whole proof, are folded; messages about the
     whole file, like that its imports are out of date, are not, and
     come first.  That message has a ‘[restart file]’ button, which does
     what ‘C-c C-r’ does.  Their terms are subterms like those of goals.
     ‘RET’ or ‘mouse-1’ on the position of a message goes there in the
     source.
   • Traces, as with ‘set_option trace.Meta.synthInstance true in’, are
     headings: ‘TAB’ on one shows its children, which Lean sends on
     request only.
   • Suggestions such as Lean's "Try this" have an ‘[apply]’ button that
     replaces the code in the source with the suggestion; this includes
     the tactics of libraries that suggest through Lean's ‘TryThis’.
     Lean's hints show the change they suggest as a diff, which applies
     the same way.  The code of an error, such as ‘[lean.invalidField]’,
     opens its explanation in Lean's reference manual.
   • ‘v’ (‘lena-infoview-view-options’) opens a menu for customizing
     what the infoview shows: which hypotheses to hide, whether to show
     the target first and the changes of the tactic at point, and how to
     show the expected type.

   • With Evil (https://github.com/emacs-evil/evil) and the ‘lena-evil’
     package, ‘(with-eval-after-load 'evil (lena-evil-setup))’ binds
     keys in normal state that don't clash with its motions: ‘]g’ and
     ‘[g’ for the next and previous goal, ‘]h’ and ‘[h’ for headings,
     ‘gr’ to refresh, ‘K’ to describe the subterm at point, and, with a
     local leader, ‘<localleader> v’ for the view options and
     ‘<localleader> y’ to copy the goal.

   Infoview, on a subterm of a goal:

   • ElDoc echoes the subterm with all its arguments made explicit, and
     its type.  With eldoc-box (https://github.com/casouri/eldoc-box),
     this appears in a popup.
   • With ‘tooltip-mode’, the tooltip of the subterm under the mouse
     shows it with its type and documentation.  We suggest turning
     native tooltips off for better formatting, by setting
     ‘use-system-tooltips’ to ‘nil’.
   • ‘RET’, ‘mouse-1’ or ‘C-h .’ (‘lena-infoview-describe’) describes
     the subterm in the help buffer ‘*Lena Help*’: its explicit form,
     its type, its documentation, and how the tactic at point changes
     it.  Its subterms can be described in turn, and the history goes
     back and forth: ‘l’ and ‘r’, or ‘C-o’ and ‘C-i’ with Evil.  In Doom
     Emacs, ‘K’ describes too.  Closing the help window returns to the
     infoview.
   • ‘M-.’ (‘xref-find-definitions’) shows the definition of the subterm
     in another window, and ‘M-,’ (‘xref-go-back’) returns to the
     infoview.  In Doom Emacs, these are ‘gd’ and ‘C-o’.

   Input method:

   • ‘C-\’ (‘toggle-input-method’) toggles the ‘Lena’ input method.
     While it is active, typing an abbreviation like ‘\a’ or ‘\to’
     inserts the corresponding character, here ‘α’ or ‘→’.  This also
     works within Isearch and the minibuffer.
   • ‘C-u C-x =’ (‘describe-char’) describes the character at point.
     While the input method is active, this includes how to type the
     character.

   Build:

   • ‘C-c C-c’ (‘project-compile’) prompts for a command to build the
     current project.  It will suggest ‘lake build’ because Lena
     configures the ‘compile-command’ variable.

   Navigation:

   • ‘M-a’ (‘forward-sentence’) go to beginning of current
     sentence/block, or to previous one.
   • ‘M-e’ (‘backward-sentence’) go to end of current sentence/block, or
     to next one.
   • ‘M-g i’ (‘imenu’) jumps to an interactively chosen definition in
     current buffer.

   Comments:

   • ‘M-;’ (‘comment-dwim’) toggles the active region as comment or, if
     no region is active, creates a comment at end of line.
   • ‘M-q’ (‘prog-fill-reindent-defun’) fills the string- or
     comment-paragraph at point, or reindents the point-surrounding
     code.


