5.2 Installation in Doom Emacs
==============================

Doom's own ‘:lang lean’ module uses upstream Nael, so disable it: in
‘~/.doom.d/init.el’, comment out ‘lean’ under ‘:lang’.  Then declare
Lena in ‘~/.doom.d/packages.el’:

     (package! lena
       :recipe (:host github :repo "ISTA-PLV/nael" :branch "lena"
                :files ("lena/*.el" "lena/lena.info" "lena/lena.texi"
                        "lena/NOTICE" "lena/LICENSE*")))
     ;; With Evil:
     (package! lena-evil
       :recipe (:host github :repo "ISTA-PLV/nael" :branch "lena"
                :files ("lena-evil/*.el" "lena-evil/LICENSE")))

   Set it up in ‘~/.doom.d/config.el’, with its keys under the local
leader ‘SPC m’, then run ‘doom sync’ and restart Emacs:

     ;; C-\ toggles Lean's Unicode input, such as \to for →, in Lean buffers.
     ;; Start the Lean server:
     (add-hook 'lena-mode-local-vars-hook #'eglot-ensure 'append)

     (map! :after lena
           :map lena-mode-map
           :localleader
           :desc "Infoview"           "i" #'lena-infoview
           :desc "Restart file"       "r" #'lena-eglot-restart-file
           :desc "Build project"      "b" #'project-compile
           :desc "ElDoc buffer"       "e" #'eldoc-doc-buffer)

     (map! :after lena-infoview-buffer
           :map lena-infoview-mode-map
           :localleader
           :desc "Refresh"            "r" #'lena-infoview-refresh
           :desc "View options"       "v" #'lena-infoview-view-options
           :desc "Copy goal"          "y" #'lena-infoview-copy-goal)

     ;; The infoview places itself, in a side window by default; see
     ;; `lena-infoview-display-action'.  The help buffer is a popup.
     (set-popup-rule! "^\\*Lena Infoview\\*" :ignore t)
     (set-popup-rule! "^\\*Lena Help\\*" :slot 2 :vslot -8 :size 0.42 :select t)

     ;; With Evil:
     (after! (evil lena-infoview-buffer)
       (lena-evil-setup))

   In the infoview's normal state, ‘]g’ and ‘[g’ go to the next and
previous goal, ‘]h’ and ‘[h’ to headings, ‘gr’ refreshes, and on a
subterm ‘K’ or ‘RET’ describes it, ‘gd’ goes to its definition and ‘C-o’
comes back.  The descriptions open in ‘*Lena Help*’, where ‘C-o’ and
‘C-i’ go back and forth and ‘q’ closes.

   To work on Lena itself, use your clone in the recipes instead, as in
‘(:local-repo "/path/to/lena" :files (...))’.


